                "text": "**Analyzing Pixel Distributions**\n\nI'm currently focused on how pixel colors are distributed within these grids. For instance, in example 1, the background is 0, pixel (1, 1) has value 3, and pixel (4, 5) has value 8. I'm noting that column 1 features a '3', and row 1 also has a '3' at position (1,1) but a 2 elsewhere, and row 4 has an 8. I'm trying to identify patterns based on the pixel placement and the background value.\n\n\n**Discovering Grid Patterns**\n\nI've made a key discovery: the intersecting point of the column containing one input value and the row containing another input value always results in a '2'. In other words, if (A,B) and (C,D) are provided, the point at (A,D) and (C,B) is always '2'. Furthermore, based on the pixel placement and the background value, the row containing the first input value is filled with '2s', except for the value in the column of the original value! The same is true for the column and row placement.\n\n\n**Unveiling Intersections**\n\nI've made a crucial observation. If the rows or columns coincide, the shared row or column is filled with '2's. Now, when two points have different rows and columns, they seem to draw lines, a cross pattern, and those lines intersect at (r_A, c_B) and (r_B, c_A). Those intersections? They're consistently colored '2', mirroring the logic when rows or columns are shared.\n\n\n**Formulating a Generalized Rule**\n\nI think I'm starting to formalize the process. The core idea seems to revolve around drawing \"crosses\" from the initial points. When I look at example 3 and 4, the initial colors for the grid's cells are kept. Any cell that lies on both crosses will take a value of '2'. I'm aiming to define an algorithm now. I think I'm almost there.\n\n\n",
